  {
    "document_id": "mplus_hypertension_symptoms",
    "source_id": "MedlinePlus",
    "source_name": "MedlinePlus Health Topics (NLM / NIH)",
    "publisher": "National Library of Medicine (NLM/NIH)",
    "title": "High Blood Pressure (Hypertension) - Symptoms",
    "content": "Topic: High Blood Pressure (Hypertension)\nSection: Symptoms\nPublisher: National Library of Medicine (NLM/NIH)\n\nHypertension is often called the 'silent killer' because it typically causes no symptoms until severe or life-threatening organ damage has occurred. In severe hypertensive crises, symptoms may include severe headaches, chest pain, shortness of breath, blurred vision, dizziness, or nosebleeds.",
