  {
    "document_id": "mplus_community_acquired_pneumonia_diagnosis_and_severity_assessment",
    "source_id": "MedlinePlus",
    "source_name": "MedlinePlus Health Topics (NLM / NIH)",
    "publisher": "National Library of Medicine (NLM/NIH)",
    "title": "Pneumonia - Diagnosis and Severity Assessment",
    "content": "Topic: Pneumonia\nSection: Diagnosis and Severity Assessment\nPublisher: National Library of Medicine (NLM/NIH)\n\nDiagnosis is confirmed by chest X-ray showing lung infiltrates/consolidation, physical examination (crackles, bronchial breath sounds), pulse oximetry, sputum culture, and blood tests. Clinical severity is commonly evaluated using the CURB-65 score (Confusion, Urea > 7 mmol/L, Respiratory rate >= 30, Blood pressure < 90/60, Age >= 65) to determine outpatient vs inpatient hospital admission.",
    "source_url": "https://medlineplus.gov/pneumonia.html",
    "document_type": "disease_summary",
    "evidence_role": "reference_summary",
    "provenance_status": "VERIFIED",
